1
00:00:00,160 --> 00:00:04,340
In the autumn of 1944, two Frenchmen were riding their bicycles along

2
00:00:04,340 --> 00:00:07,740
a quiet country road not far from the front lines.

3
00:00:07,960 --> 00:00:10,960
Under a line of trees, they noticed something the war had made

4
00:00:10,960 --> 00:00:12,240
very familiar.

5
00:00:12,660 --> 00:00:17,020
American trucks covered with netting and behind them a row of Sherman

6
00:00:17,020 --> 00:00:17,840
tanks.

7
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:21,640
An American sentry stepped out to wave them along, but the two

8
00:00:21,640 --> 00:00:23,620
men were not looking at him anymore.

9
00:00:23,720 --> 00:00:25,720
They were staring past his shoulder.

10
00:00:26,080 --> 00:00:29,960
Because there, in the field, four young soldiers had walked up to

11
00:00:29,960 --> 00:00:34,060
one of those tanks, bent down, and lifted it off the ground.

12
00:00:34,220 --> 00:00:37,640
A tank like that weighs more than thirty tons, and yet the

13
00:00:37,640 --> 00:00:41,520
four men carried it, turned it, and set it down again, as

14
00:00:41,520 --> 00:00:43,720
easily as you might move a kitchen table.

15
00:00:43,880 --> 00:00:47,020
One of the Frenchmen turned to his friend and said in complete

16
00:00:47,020 --> 00:00:51,360
seriousness, These Americans, they are very strong.

17
00:00:51,500 --> 00:00:53,200
The sentry did not correct him.

18
00:00:53,300 --> 00:00:54,460
He could not.

19
00:00:54,760 --> 00:00:57,400
Because what those two men had just seen was one of the

20
00:00:57,400 --> 00:01:00,080
best kept secrets of the Second World War.

21
00:01:00,220 --> 00:01:01,960
The tank was made of rubber.

22
00:01:02,040 --> 00:01:03,580
It was full of air.

23
00:01:03,720 --> 00:01:07,300
And the soldiers who carried it belonged to a unit that officially

24
00:01:07,300 --> 00:01:08,000
did not exist.

25
00:01:08,940 --> 00:01:11,420
Today, we call them the Ghost Army.

26
00:01:11,740 --> 00:01:14,880
And for more than 50 years, almost nobody knew what they did.

27
00:01:15,720 --> 00:01:19,200
To understand the Ghost Army, we have to go back to January

28
00:01:19,200 --> 00:01:23,200
of 1944 to a training camp in Tennessee called Camp Forest.

29
00:01:24,180 --> 00:01:28,100
That winter, the United States Army quietly created a new unit with

30
00:01:28,100 --> 00:01:33,200
a long, forgettable name, the 23rd Headquarters Special Troops.

31
00:01:33,360 --> 00:01:37,260
It had about 1 ,100 men, and it had one job, unlike

32
00:01:37,260 --> 00:01:38,900
any other job in the Army.

33
00:01:39,040 --> 00:01:40,940
It was not supposed to fight the enemy.

34
00:01:41,080 --> 00:01:42,800
It was supposed to fool him.

35
00:01:42,940 --> 00:01:45,920
The idea was simple and a little bit crazy.

36
00:01:46,180 --> 00:01:49,240
If you could make the Germans believe that a large American force

37
00:01:49,240 --> 00:01:52,880
was in one place, then the real army could move somewhere else,

38
00:01:53,100 --> 00:01:54,680
where it was not expected.

39
00:01:54,920 --> 00:01:57,580
Wars are won and lost on information.

40
00:01:57,820 --> 00:01:59,080
Where is the enemy?

41
00:01:59,320 --> 00:02:00,720
How strong is he?

42
00:02:00,900 --> 00:02:02,660
Where will he strike next?

43
00:02:02,900 --> 00:02:05,720
The Ghost Army's mission was to make sure the Germans got all

44
00:02:05,940 --> 00:02:07,180
three answers wrong.

45
00:02:07,420 --> 00:02:10,960
But to do that, the Army needed a very special kind of

46
00:02:10,960 --> 00:02:11,480
soldier.

47
00:02:11,700 --> 00:02:15,740
So recruiters went looking in places the military does not usually visit.

48
00:02:15,880 --> 00:02:21,800
Art schools in New York and Philadelphia, advertising agencies, design studios, theater

49
00:02:21,800 --> 00:02:22,560
companies.

50
00:02:22,700 --> 00:02:28,460
They found painters and sculptors, architects and set designers, young men who

51
00:02:28,460 --> 00:02:31,780
knew how to make something look real, when it wasn't.

52
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:35,260
Among them was a young man from Indiana who loved fashion.

53
00:02:35,440 --> 00:02:38,940
His name was Bill Blass, and one day he would become one

54
00:02:38,940 --> 00:02:41,560
of the most famous designers in America.

55
00:02:41,700 --> 00:02:45,400
There was a quiet young man named Ellsworth Kelly, who would later

56
00:02:45,400 --> 00:02:48,340
become one of the great painters of the 20th century.

57
00:02:48,520 --> 00:02:52,600
There was Arthur Singer, who would spend his life painting birds so

58
00:02:52,600 --> 00:02:55,980
precisely that his work filled field guides for generations.

59
00:02:56,180 --> 00:02:59,160
And there were hundreds of others, men who would go on to

60
00:02:59,160 --> 00:03:03,300
become photographers, illustrators, art teachers and engineers.

61
00:03:03,480 --> 00:03:06,440
Many of them had never imagined themselves in uniform.

62
00:03:06,600 --> 00:03:10,140
Now they were being trained to deceive the most powerful army in

63
00:03:10,140 --> 00:03:15,280
Europe, using paint, rubber, sound, and imagination.

64
00:03:15,660 --> 00:03:18,400
The Ghost Army fought with three kinds of weapons.

65
00:03:18,600 --> 00:03:20,740
None of them could fire a single bullet.

66
00:03:20,880 --> 00:03:23,680
The first weapon was what the enemy could see.

67
00:03:23,840 --> 00:03:28,000
The largest part of the unit was the 603rd Engineer Camouflage Battalion.

68
00:03:29,020 --> 00:03:30,200
These were the artists.

69
00:03:30,400 --> 00:03:36,000
Their tools were inflatable decoys, rubber tanks, trucks, jeeps, and artillery guns.

70
00:03:36,520 --> 00:03:39,900
Each one folded into a bag and blown up with a compressor

71
00:03:39,900 --> 00:03:41,000
in a matter of minutes.

72
00:03:41,520 --> 00:03:44,900
From the air, through a pair of binoculars, or in a reconnaissance

73
00:03:45,180 --> 00:03:49,460
photograph, a field full of these decoys looked exactly like an armored

74
00:03:49,460 --> 00:03:51,200
division getting ready for battle.

75
00:03:51,380 --> 00:03:54,820
But the artists knew that an army is more than its vehicles.

76
00:03:55,000 --> 00:03:57,320
Real tanks leave tracks in the mud.

77
00:03:57,640 --> 00:04:02,340
Real soldiers leave trash, laundry, and campfire smoke, so the men of

78
00:04:02,340 --> 00:04:04,420
the Ghost Army made those too.

79
00:04:04,580 --> 00:04:08,800
They used vehicles and even bulldozers to draw tank tracks across the

80
00:04:08,800 --> 00:04:09,420
fields.

81
00:04:09,640 --> 00:04:13,300
They placed decoys with care as if a real crew had parked

82
00:04:13,300 --> 00:04:14,280
them in a hurry.

83
00:04:14,420 --> 00:04:16,900
The second weapon was what the enemy could hear.

84
00:04:17,020 --> 00:04:22,260
A separate company, the 3132nd Signal Service Company, had trained in secret

85
00:04:22,260 --> 00:04:23,840
in upstate New York.

86
00:04:24,040 --> 00:04:27,520
Working with engineers from Bell Labs, they had recorded the sounds of

87
00:04:27,520 --> 00:04:33,720
real American units, tanks rolling, trucks shifting gears, men building bridges, officers

88
00:04:33,720 --> 00:04:34,740
shouting orders.

89
00:04:34,900 --> 00:04:38,800
They mixed those sounds into programs, like a radio play, and stored

90
00:04:38,800 --> 00:04:43,240
them on wire recorders, the most advanced recording technology of the day.

91
00:04:43,440 --> 00:04:47,820
At night they drove half tracks close to the front, carrying enormous

92
00:04:47,820 --> 00:04:51,360
500 watt speakers, and then they pressed play.

93
00:04:51,740 --> 00:04:55,440
On a quiet night, those sounds could carry for miles, some say

94
00:04:55,440 --> 00:04:57,200
as far as 15 miles.

95
00:04:57,580 --> 00:05:00,720
To a German soldier listening in the dark, it sounded like an

96
00:05:00,720 --> 00:05:03,200
entire armored column was moving into position.

97
00:05:03,560 --> 00:05:06,520
The third weapon was what the enemy could intercept.

98
00:05:06,680 --> 00:05:09,340
The Signal Company special handled the radio.

99
00:05:09,640 --> 00:05:12,600
Every army listened to its enemy's radio traffic.

100
00:05:12,760 --> 00:05:15,540
The Ghost Army used that against the Germans.

101
00:05:15,680 --> 00:05:20,040
Its operators sent fake messages, imitating the real units they were pretending

102
00:05:20,040 --> 00:05:20,880
to be.

103
00:05:21,040 --> 00:05:24,720
They even learned to copy the personal rhythm of other operators, the

104
00:05:24,720 --> 00:05:29,320
way each man tapped out Morse code, almost like handwriting, so that

105
00:05:29,320 --> 00:05:33,320
a German listener, hearing a familiar hand on the air, would never

106
00:05:33,320 --> 00:05:34,480
suspect a thing.

107
00:05:34,760 --> 00:05:37,860
And there was a fourth weapon, maybe the most human of all.

108
00:05:38,180 --> 00:05:38,780
Theater.

109
00:05:39,000 --> 00:05:42,300
Members of the Ghost Army sewed on the shoulder patches of other

110
00:05:42,300 --> 00:05:42,640
divisions.

111
00:05:42,940 --> 00:05:45,620
They painted fake unit markings on their trucks.

112
00:05:45,800 --> 00:05:49,420
Some of them went into nearby towns and sat in cafes, talking

113
00:05:49,420 --> 00:05:53,300
a little too loudly about where their division was headed next, knowing

114
00:05:53,300 --> 00:05:55,060
that spies might be listening.

115
00:05:55,220 --> 00:05:59,040
A few officers even played the part of generals, riding through villages

116
00:05:59,040 --> 00:06:04,200
with the right flags on their cars, sight, sound, radio, and a

117
00:06:04,200 --> 00:06:05,380
bit of acting.

118
00:06:05,520 --> 00:06:09,660
Put together it was the first traveling multimedia deception unit in the

119
00:06:09,660 --> 00:06:12,140
history of the United States Army.

120
00:06:12,820 --> 00:06:16,760
In the spring of 1944, the Ghost Army crossed the Atlantic.

121
00:06:16,920 --> 00:06:19,640
Some of its men landed in France in the weeks after D

122
00:06:19,640 --> 00:06:22,680
-Day while the fighting in Normandy was still raging.

123
00:06:22,800 --> 00:06:26,020
Their first missions were small and they learned quickly.

124
00:06:26,200 --> 00:06:29,220
A decoy placed in the wrong spot could give the whole game

125
00:06:29,220 --> 00:06:29,660
away.

126
00:06:29,900 --> 00:06:33,260
A rubber tank left in the sun could slowly sag.

127
00:06:33,480 --> 00:06:37,080
A careless remark in a village could undo a week of work.

128
00:06:37,340 --> 00:06:42,300
Every operation became a production, with a script, a set, a cast,

129
00:06:42,480 --> 00:06:43,520
and a schedule.

130
00:06:43,640 --> 00:06:47,060
The men joked that they were a traveling roadshow, in a way

131
00:06:47,060 --> 00:06:51,160
they were, and their stages were never far from real danger.

132
00:06:51,260 --> 00:06:54,500
To be convincing, the Ghost Army often had to set up close

133
00:06:54,500 --> 00:06:58,720
to the front line, sometimes closer than the units they were imitating.

134
00:06:58,840 --> 00:07:01,960
They had almost no real weapons to defend themselves.

135
00:07:02,080 --> 00:07:04,920
Their protection was the illusion itself.

136
00:07:05,120 --> 00:07:09,380
In late August of 1944, the Ghost Army put on its first

137
00:07:09,380 --> 00:07:10,600
full performance.

138
00:07:10,920 --> 00:07:14,520
American forces were attacking the port city of Brest on the western

139
00:07:14,520 --> 00:07:15,760
tip of France.

140
00:07:15,860 --> 00:07:19,280
The Germans there were well dug in, and the Americans did not

141
00:07:19,280 --> 00:07:22,360
have as many troops nearby as they would have liked.

142
00:07:22,560 --> 00:07:25,300
So the Ghost Army was asked to make them look bigger.

143
00:07:25,480 --> 00:07:28,320
For about a week, the unit used everything it had.

144
00:07:28,460 --> 00:07:30,720
Rubber tanks appeared in the fields.

145
00:07:30,980 --> 00:07:34,140
Sound trucks played the noise of armor moving at night.

146
00:07:34,440 --> 00:07:37,580
Radio operators filled the air with the chatter of units that were

147
00:07:37,580 --> 00:07:38,740
not really there.

148
00:07:38,920 --> 00:07:41,920
For the men, it was a test of whether all those months

149
00:07:41,920 --> 00:07:43,840
of training actually worked.

150
00:07:44,000 --> 00:07:47,080
Could a few hundred artists really make an enemy see an army

151
00:07:47,080 --> 00:07:48,660
that did not exist?

152
00:07:48,860 --> 00:07:51,400
The answer, it turned out, was yes.

153
00:07:51,600 --> 00:07:55,020
And the army began to call on them again and again.

154
00:07:55,200 --> 00:07:59,440
One of the most dangerous moments came in September of 1944, in

155
00:07:59,440 --> 00:08:01,580
the small country of Luxembourg.

156
00:08:01,780 --> 00:08:04,780
General Patton's Third Army was pushing east.

157
00:08:04,780 --> 00:08:08,540
But as the troops moved, a gap opened in the American line,

158
00:08:08,760 --> 00:08:12,260
a stretch of almost seventy miles that was barely defended.

159
00:08:12,480 --> 00:08:15,520
If the Germans had discovered that gap, they could have poured through

160
00:08:15,760 --> 00:08:18,780
it and struck the American forces from behind.

161
00:08:19,020 --> 00:08:21,740
There was no time to bring in a real division to fill

162
00:08:21,740 --> 00:08:26,080
it, so the Ghost army was sent instead, disguised as the Sixth

163
00:08:26,080 --> 00:08:27,260
Armored Division.

164
00:08:27,440 --> 00:08:31,520
For about a week, near a town called Bettenburg, a few hundred

165
00:08:31,520 --> 00:08:33,660
men pretended to be thousands.

166
00:08:33,840 --> 00:08:37,760
Inflatable tanks were set up in the woods, radio operators played the

167
00:08:37,760 --> 00:08:41,200
part of a busy armored headquarters, and for four nights in a

168
00:08:41,200 --> 00:08:44,860
row, the sound trucks rolled out and filled the darkness with the

169
00:08:44,860 --> 00:08:46,740
noise of tanks on the move.

170
00:08:46,920 --> 00:08:49,820
It was, in every sense, a bluff.

171
00:08:49,980 --> 00:08:53,620
If the Germans had attacked, there was almost nothing behind the curtain.

172
00:08:53,780 --> 00:08:55,320
But they did not attack.

173
00:08:55,500 --> 00:09:00,240
The illusion held, until real troops could arrive and close the gap.

174
00:09:00,480 --> 00:09:03,040
Even in the middle of all this, the men of the Ghost

175
00:09:03,040 --> 00:09:05,120
Army never stopped being artists.

176
00:09:05,380 --> 00:09:08,980
When they were not inflating tanks or driving sound trucks, many of

177
00:09:08,980 --> 00:09:09,920
them were drawing.

178
00:09:10,300 --> 00:09:12,580
They sketched each other's sleeping and barns.

179
00:09:12,840 --> 00:09:17,080
They painted the ruined churches of Normandy, the streets of Paris, the

180
00:09:17,080 --> 00:09:21,520
farm women of Luxembourg, the long gray roads of the winter of

181
00:09:21,520 --> 00:09:22,400
1944.

182
00:09:22,940 --> 00:09:26,260
Hundreds of these sketches and watercolors survived the war.

183
00:09:26,560 --> 00:09:29,620
Today they are some of the most personal records we have of

184
00:09:29,620 --> 00:09:32,740
what it felt like to be a young American soldier in Europe.

185
00:09:32,980 --> 00:09:37,200
Not a heroic poster, but a tired boy with a pencil, trying

186
00:09:37,200 --> 00:09:40,700
to remember a place he would never see again.

187
00:09:41,240 --> 00:09:45,700
By March of 1945 the war in Europe was nearing its end.

188
00:09:45,900 --> 00:09:49,960
One great obstacle remained between the Allies and the heart of Germany,

189
00:09:50,060 --> 00:09:51,760
the Rhine River.

190
00:09:52,180 --> 00:09:55,480
Crossing a river that wide, under fire, is one of the most

191
00:09:55,480 --> 00:09:57,720
dangerous things an army can attempt.

192
00:09:57,880 --> 00:10:01,620
If the Germans knew exactly where the crossing would happen, they could

193
00:10:01,620 --> 00:10:04,400
concentrate everything they had in that one place.

194
00:10:04,560 --> 00:10:08,080
So the Ghost Army was given its largest and final mission, known

195
00:10:08,080 --> 00:10:09,780
as Operation Viersen.

196
00:10:10,000 --> 00:10:13,340
Its task was to convince the Germans that the American 9th Army

197
00:10:13,340 --> 00:10:17,320
would cross the Rhine at one place and time, when in fact,

198
00:10:17,420 --> 00:10:19,400
it would cross somewhere else.

199
00:10:19,560 --> 00:10:22,900
For this performance, the Ghost Army pulled out every trick it had

200
00:10:22,900 --> 00:10:23,440
learned.

201
00:10:23,760 --> 00:10:27,000
Hundreds of inflatable decoys, fake headquarters,

202
00:10:27,600 --> 00:10:32,280
radio traffic for whole divisions that were really somewhere else, sound programs

203
00:10:32,280 --> 00:10:35,000
of bridge building equipment and heavy vehicles at night.

204
00:10:35,640 --> 00:10:39,040
And this time, they had extra help from other units to make

205
00:10:39,040 --> 00:10:40,000
the show even bigger.

206
00:10:40,840 --> 00:10:44,480
When the real crossing began, the resistance was lighter than many had

207
00:10:44,480 --> 00:10:45,340
feared.

208
00:10:45,560 --> 00:10:49,020
The Ninth Army crossed the Rhine, and the road into Germany was

209
00:10:49,020 --> 00:10:49,800
open.

210
00:10:50,040 --> 00:10:53,020
Two months later, the war in Europe was over.

211
00:10:53,260 --> 00:10:56,100
When the men of the Ghost Army came home, they did something

212
00:10:56,100 --> 00:10:58,640
that might be the hardest part of their whole story.

213
00:10:58,780 --> 00:11:00,060
They said nothing.

214
00:11:00,180 --> 00:11:02,000
Their work was classified.

215
00:11:02,160 --> 00:11:05,160
They were told not to talk about it, and they did not.

216
00:11:05,320 --> 00:11:06,660
Not to their friends.

217
00:11:06,960 --> 00:11:09,320
Often not even to their own wives and children.

218
00:11:09,480 --> 00:11:10,800
They went back to their lives.

219
00:11:11,040 --> 00:11:16,040
They became painters, designers, architects, teachers, photographers.

220
00:11:16,300 --> 00:11:18,840
Some became famous, most did not.

221
00:11:19,040 --> 00:11:22,080
And for decades, if anyone asked what they did in the war,

222
00:11:22,160 --> 00:11:25,360
many of them simply said they had been in the engineers.

223
00:11:25,560 --> 00:11:29,480
It was not until 1996 that the Ghost Army's story was officially

224
00:11:29,480 --> 00:11:30,780
declassified.

225
00:11:30,960 --> 00:11:34,580
By then, many of its men had already passed away, their families

226
00:11:34,580 --> 00:11:36,320
never knowing the full truth.

227
00:11:36,480 --> 00:11:39,800
In the years that followed, the families and researchers who helped bring

228
00:11:39,800 --> 00:11:42,960
the story to light began a long effort to have the unit

229
00:11:42,960 --> 00:11:43,700
honored.

230
00:11:43,860 --> 00:11:47,980
In 2022, a law was signed to award the Ghost Army the

231
00:11:47,980 --> 00:11:52,360
Congressional Gold Medal, the highest civilian honor Congress can give.

232
00:11:52,560 --> 00:11:57,500
And in March of 2024, at a ceremony in Washington, the medal

233
00:11:57,500 --> 00:11:59,140
was finally presented.

234
00:11:59,440 --> 00:12:02,540
Only a handful of the veterans were still alive to see it.

235
00:12:02,720 --> 00:12:05,140
How many lives did the Ghost Army save?

236
00:12:05,320 --> 00:12:07,000
We will never know exactly.

237
00:12:07,220 --> 00:12:11,820
Historians estimate the number somewhere between 15 ,000 and 30 ,000.

238
00:12:12,400 --> 00:12:15,900
15 to 30 ,000 young men who came home because the enemy

239
00:12:15,900 --> 00:12:18,300
was looking the wrong way at the right moment.

240
00:12:18,580 --> 00:12:21,740
And maybe that is the most remarkable thing about this story.

241
00:12:21,880 --> 00:12:24,760
The Ghost Army did not win its battles with weapons.

242
00:12:24,920 --> 00:12:28,620
It won them with imagination, with the talents of people who in

243
00:12:28,620 --> 00:12:32,760
any other time would have spent their lives painting, designing and making

244
00:12:32,760 --> 00:12:33,940
beautiful things.

245
00:12:34,140 --> 00:12:37,060
So the next time you see an old watercolour of a French

246
00:12:37,060 --> 00:12:41,480
village, or a dress by Bill Blass, or a painting by Ellsworth

247
00:12:41,480 --> 00:12:45,240
Kelly in a museum, Remember the rubber tanks in the fields of

248
00:12:45,240 --> 00:12:45,860
France.

249
00:12:46,020 --> 00:12:50,180
And remember those two Frenchmen on their bicycles watching four young Americans

250
00:12:50,180 --> 00:12:51,740
lift a tank into the air.

251
00:12:51,900 --> 00:12:53,860
They were right, in a way.

